  "body": "The spec says:\n> When comparing a CSS element [type selector](https://drafts.csswg.org/selectors/#type-selector) to the names of [HTML elements](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/infrastructure.html#html-elements) in [HTML documents](https://dom.spec.whatwg.org/#html-document), the CSS element [type selector](https://drafts.csswg.org/selectors/#type-selector) must first be [converted to ASCII lowercase](https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#ascii-lowercase). The same selector when compared to other elements must be compared according to its original case. In both cases, to match, the values must be [identical to](https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#string-is) each other (and therefore the comparison is case sensitive).\n\nIs this mean CSS's syntax is lowercased, HTML's tag/attribute name is lowercased, and then a case-sensitive match is performed?If so,why not add a sentence to more clearly  explain how it compare",
